<article class="env-definition" id="1C005C">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/1C005C">Definition <span data-tag="1C005C">6.2.2.1</span>.</a>sievesieve!covering Let $\calC $ be a $\infty $-category. A <em>sieve</em> on $\calC $ is a full subcategory of $\calC ^{(0)} \subseteq \calC $ having the property that if $f: C \rightarrow D$ is a morphism in $\calC $, and $D$ belongs to $\calC ^{(0)}$, then $C$ also belongs to $\calC ^{(0)}$.calC0@$\calC ^{(0)}$ </p>
  <p>Observe that if $f: \calC \rightarrow \calD $ is a functor between $\infty $-categories and $\calD ^{(0)} \subseteq \calD $ is a sieve on $\calD $, then $f^{-1} \calD ^{(0)} = \calD ^{(0)} \times _{\calD } \calC $ is a sieve on $\calC $. Moreover, if $f$ is an equivalence, then $f^{-1}$ induces a bijection between sieves on $\calD $ and sieves on $\calC $. </p>
  <p>If $C \in \calC $ is an object, then a <em>sieve on $C$</em> is a sieve on the $\infty $-category $\calC _{/C}$. Given a morphism $f: D \rightarrow C$ and a sieve $\calC ^{(0)}_{/C}$ on $C$, we let $f^{\ast } \calC ^{(0)}_{/C}$ denote the unique sieve on $D$ such that $f^{\ast } \calC ^{(0)}_{/C} \subseteq \calC _{/D}$ and $\calC ^{(0)}_{/C}$ determine same sieve on $\calC _{/f}$. </p>
  <p>A <em>Grothendieck topology</em>Grothendieck topology on an $\infty $-category $\calC $ consists of a specification, for each object $C$ of $\calC $, of a collection of sieves on $C$, which we will refer to as <em>covering sieves</em>. The collections of covering sieves are required to possess the following properties: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
If $C$ is an object of $\calC $, then the sieve $\calC _{/C} \subseteq \calC _{/C}$ on $C$ is a covering sieve. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
If $f: C \rightarrow D$ is a morphism in $\calC $, and $\calC ^{(0)}_{/D}$ is a covering sieve on $D$, then $f^{\ast } \calC ^{(0)}_{/C}$ is a covering sieve on $C$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(3)$"><p>
Let $C$ be an object of $\calC $, $\calC ^{(0)}_{/C}$ a covering sieve on $C$, and $\calC ^{(1)}_{/C}$ an arbitrary sieve on $C$. Suppose that, for each $f: D \rightarrow C$ belonging to the sieve $\calC ^{(0)}_{/C}$, the pullback $f^{\ast } \calC ^{(1)}_{/C}$ is a covering sieve on $D$. Then $\calC ^{(1)}_{/C}$ is a covering sieve on $C$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
